{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And He said, 'I shall be.'</b> It is difficult: why do I need all these 'and He said's? And the answer: the Holy One, blessed be He, called to Moses: 'אהיה is My name,' because 'I shall be' forever and ever; and 'אשר אהיה' — this means 'of אהיה.' Then the Holy One, blessed be He, said further: perhaps Moses will suppose that My name is 'אהיה אשר אהיה' — therefore He went back and said 'כה תאמר.' And 'He said further': perhaps Israel will yet call Me 'אהיה,' and they will be mistaken, since it should be read 'יהיה' for eternity — therefore it is written 'ויאמר עוד אלהים לו,' which is written with י-ה, which implies 'יהיה.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The comment appears to interpret the spelling of אלהים as containing the letters י-ה (yod-heh), from which it derives the reading יהיה; the logic turns on the presence of these two divine-name letters within the word."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:38.453407875Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}